A||N||8||contra||How do i fix my sagging roof fabric?||||1||
Z||000000||contra||07-20-2000||02:55 PM||||I dont have a sunroof on my 87GT so i've go the full roof liner up there. Well, as i've heard it would happen, my fabric has finally given way and balooned out instead of sticking to the roof. I really dont have the patience or time to take the roof liner out unless it's a REALLY easy job. I was wondering if i just slit the fabric somewhere where nobody would see it, like inside the lip by the molding on the roof, and then sprayed glue in there, would that work? If so, what kind of glue should i get? Contact cement spray i think would eat the foam inside, so i'd want something that would stick, and not seep through if possible<BR>thanx<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||07-20-2000||03:35 PM||||Those foam GM headliners are only good for 10-15 years. The trouble is, the foam backing is rapidly deteriorating, so any adhesive will not last long because the foam continues to just fall apart. I had mine replaced at an upholstry shop for $100, looks like new.||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||07-20-2000||03:41 PM||||Contra, I have replaced the headliner in everyone of my Fieros..it was one of the first projects I ever tackled on my own...and the easiest!<BR>First things first...get the fabric.<BR>I got mine at Hancock Fabrics..most all fabric stores should carry it. It was actually on a bin labeled "Automotive headliners". They had EVERY color, and then some! I bought both the grey and tan. Its the exact same thing, foam backed fabric. Even better was the fact that it cost $7 a yard!<BR>After you get your fabric and glue (I used 3M spray adhesive, any hardware store carries it, its in a big black spray can, cost around $10 (more than the fabric!), then get to work on the interior.<BR>You'll need to take off the 3rd brake light cover (if applicable).<BR>Then pull down the seatbelt bolt covers, and take out the seatbelt bolts by the seat headrest. Youll need a torx driver or bit thats a T-47(I believe thats the size, pretty sure). Pull out the inside said panels, youll need to take out a small phillips head screw inside the door jam, easy. Youll prob. break the plastic tab when you pull out the sail panel, not a factor, any auto parts store will stock them.<BR>After you pull ou the inner sail panels, pull down the plastic trim pieces that run along the inside over the side windows...they pull right out, just take care in doing it.<BR>Take off the close hanger hooks and dome light assembly up front.<BR>Now, CAREFULLY pull down the headliner board, its usually brittle, so you may want a helper.<BR>Once thats out, pull of the old fabric. Take a brush and clean off all the "fuzz" of the headliner board.<BR>Now, lay the board on a table and generously spray the board AND the fabric and lay the fabric down over the board...smooth it out.(The can will say to only spray on either the board or the fabric, but trust me, spray both.)<BR>After about 20 minutes, trim the excess fabric (not too short). Re-apply in reverse order.<BR>It sounds like alot, but believe me, its really easy. The first time I did it, it took about an hour, now I can do it all from start to finish in about 30 minutes (and thats WITH the sunroof).<BR>It really takes very little effort, very little money (dont ever pay the 100+ dollars specialty stores charge...pu-leez!) will look factory new!<BR>I think I included everything..its been several months since Ive done the last one, but Im pretty sure thats it! You'll be happy w/ the results, believe me!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> p.s. I'll post some pics tonight or tomorrow......<p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 07-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DXR_DAD||07-20-2000||03:45 PM||||Cantra, lots of info on this, use the search<BR>Heres one:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001610.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/001610.html</A> <P>Be like NIKE "JUST DO IT"<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||3
Z||000004||contra||07-20-2000||05:21 PM||||thanx my7fieros!<BR>i'll probably tackle the job this sunday<BR>i figure i will need a couple hours cause i'll be taking apart the inside for the first time heheh, but it's all good. I'm guessing i dont have to use the same fabric that was on there originally? Cause i might want to try something different. I was thinking of fiberglassing the whole thing, but i dunno if it'll look so nice, or i might pass out from the fumes inside the car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000005||batboy||07-20-2000||07:00 PM||||Yep, they sure make it sound easy. My7 Fieros forgot to tell you that half the time the board is broken or you crack it during removal. They can be repaired, but it takes more time. I do almost all my own wrenching on my Fieros, but the headliner is not mechanical work. I can't whack it with a hammer or tweak it with vice-grips (ok, maybe I could, but it'd leave marks). Installing a headliner is more like artwork, if you want it looking great anyway. Since it'd look like guano if I did it myself, I farm out the work. Besides, I like to keep those auto upholstry folks in business. Never know when I might need a tuck and roll interior done on a streetrod.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 07-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||My7Fieros||07-20-2000||07:48 PM||||Cool, it IS easy Contra....Ive done it on 9 Fieros....never had a prob, and looks better than new. I'll dig up some pics......<BR>oh yeah, and it only cost around 20 bucks.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Gokart||07-20-2000||08:05 PM||||Did mine last month following these instructions <A HREF="http://www.castlegate.net/personals/daveheld/TechArt/headlinr.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.castlegate.net/personals/daveheld/TechArt/headlinr.htm</A>  Piece of cake <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Especially if you lay down about 3 coats of polyurethane on each side of the headliner board itself before you glue the material to it. Makes it very rigid when putting it back in! Good luck.<P>------------------<BR> Marc                     87GT||||reg||14
Z||000008||Carrolles||07-21-2000||08:35 AM||||I've done several through the years and they aren't difficult at all.  Be sure the spray adhesive is made for foam backed fabric.  Regular spray glue will let go in a day or two.<P>For a cheap temporary repair go to WalMart and buy a couple of packs of Upholstry Twist Pins.  There are about ten in a pack for about 89 cents.  They look like a clear plastic button with a corkscrew shaped pin.  Simply twist it into the headliner.<P>You can be creative with the pattern to make it look somewhat like tuck and roll.  You can also spray paint the plastic button parts to match the fabric so they are not as noticable.<P>Like I said, this is a good temporary fix (however I did an old Safari van once and sold it with the repair after three years).<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
